surveys.afterRound:
After steps 8, 8b, 8c, 10 and 9 and the post-round audio step, the game-facing picture changes in four places:
(1) Physics becomes reachable. Step 8b connects PhysicsAPI (events, sensors, raycast, AABB/circle queries, isGrounded) in every mode and settles how an app loads the physics build. Modes A/B get queries in whatever form 8b picks (async, a one-tick-old snapshot, or Mode C only), and the collider-generation fix may or may not be included.
(2) Audio stops hanging on WebKit and gains audio.state and a first-gesture unlock hook.
(3) In Mode A, device loss reaches onDeviceLost and an empty world renders (step 10). The camera stays frozen, and there are still no textures and no particles.
(4) Textured quads, lines and beziers show their images upright (step 8).
Steps 8c (SDF padding) and 9 (docs, adversarial review, Chrome/Safari GPU legs) do not touch gameplay.
Everything else stays as described:
- no sprite sheets, animation, tint or texture unloading, within a 1020-image, 512 px session budget;
- mirrored sprites culled;
- no gamepad, touch or action mapping, plus the stuck-key and auto-repeat bugs;
- no render interpolation, fixed-rate gameplay hook or time scale;
- the y-down, pixel-unit physics defaults under a y-up 20-unit camera, with no TS configuration;
- no velocity control, rotation lock, one-way platforms, polygon or tile colliders, or ray filters;
- no level lifecycle; compact() is a no-op;
- no production save/load or state readback;
- SFX-only audio;
- no working text, UI or localization layer;
- dead config fields.
Net result: after the round Hyperion is a capable 2D renderer on top of a well-tested Rust physics core, with a thin game-facing layer. It fits a vertical slice or prototype in Mode B/C, for a team ready to fork the engine for sprite animation and mirroring and to write the input, level, save, music and UI layers itself. It is not ready, as is, for a complete, complex 2D game.

surveys.summary:
Hyperion's game-facing layer is thin next to its renderer.

What is solid:
- a fluent, write-only EntityHandle (2D/3D spawn, depth, hierarchy, lights);
- safe id reuse;
- a fixed 60 Hz Rust simulation with a well-tested Rapier2D core;
- isolated per-frame hooks;
- basic keyboard/pointer polling and SFX playback with 2D panning.

Today physics is unreachable: PhysicsAPI is never connected to the WASM, and the physics WASM is never loaded. Step 8b fixes that, with the Mode A/B query form still open.

After the round, a complete 2D game still lacks:
- sprite sheets and animation, held back by no UV rect, no tint, a 1020-image session cap at ≤512 px and no unloading (the remaining blocker);
- mirrored sprites, which disappear under cullMode 'back';
- gamepad, touch and action mapping, plus stuck-key and auto-repeat bugs;
- render interpolation (60 Hz ticks under per-frame variable-dt hooks);
- physics defaults that suit a y-down pixel world, while the renderer is y-up with a 20-unit view, and that TS cannot configure;
- key body controls (velocity, rotation lock, one-way platforms, more shapes, ray filters);
- any level lifecycle (no clear world, orphaned children, a no-op compact);
- production save/load or state readback;
- music streaming and audio buses;
- text, UI and localization.

On top of that, the default Mode A on Chromium/Electron freezes the camera and refuses textures.

The round fixes only the physics wiring (8b), the Mode A empty world and device-loss forwarding (10), and WebKit audio suspend/unlock (post-round). Readiness for a complete, complex 2D game after the round is therefore not ready. It is adequate for a Mode B/C prototype or vertical slice, by a team that budgets engine work for the sprite path and mirroring and writes its own input, level, save, music and UI layers.

