The mirrored-quad defect (cullMode 'back', forward-pass.ts:271, 285) has a pending fix 'da decidere col proprietario' (step-8 plan :4482), which will be one more change. CommandTape is always version 1 (command-tape.ts:39-48).

surveys.afterRound:
After steps 8, 8b, 8c, 10 and 9 and the WebKit audio step, this dimension changes only at the edges.
- **Step 8** adds the dev tool `engine.debug.textureTiers()` and a dev warning for sprite lights without a texture. It also draws every tier image upright, which changes how existing textured quads, lines and beziers look, along with their tapes and snapshots.
- **Step 8b** is the only distribution change. It wires PhysicsAPI to the WASM in every mode. It also decides how an app and the harness get the physics build: a config option, a build alias or the harness on the physics build. That makes collision events and queries reachable, and the F3 wireframes too, if that build carries physics-debug. Its exit criteria cover neither a dev-tools variant nor the isolation of user collision callbacks.
- **Step 8c** is rendering only.
- **Step 10** makes a Mode A device loss reach `config.onDeviceLost`, makes an empty Mode A world render, and corrects the masterplan's claim of transparent device-lost recovery. Recovery itself stays manual.
- **Step 9** refreshes CLAUDE.md and the internal docs listed in round plan §5. It also adds tooling for engine developers: a Safari section in /gpu-check, a committed safaridriver driver and the Safari legs.
- **The WebKit audio step** adds `audio.state` and a first-gesture unlock hook.

Nothing in the round does any of the following:
- fix the failing production build (`npm run build`, iife workers);
- add a licence, a package or library build, user documentation or examples;
- add a headless test path or CI;
- fix the ECS inspector, or bring the debug camera, overlays, textures and camera sync to Mode A;
- wire replay or snapshots into the facade;
- implement or remove the seven ignored config fields and the stub methods;
- surface WASM-rejected or skewed commands, Mode B init failures or renderer failures, or isolate the frame tick;
- extend the asset pipeline, or verify the KTX2/Basis path;
- introduce versioning, or provide editor and level tooling.

The mirrored-quad fix (cullMode 'back') is left to an owner decision outside the round.

What the round changes: Step 8 adds the sprite (cookie) light in Modes B/C, draws tier images upright, and stops lights that cannot draw from opening SDF sets. Step 8b wires PhysicsAPI to the WASM. Step 8c decides SDF padding and stops point/spot marches at the SDF exit. Step 10 makes Mode A render an empty world and forwards Mode A device loss to config.onDeviceLost, and corrects masterplan lines 232/247. Step 9 refreshes the docs, runs the adversarial review, re-runs /gpu-check on the M2 in Chrome and Safari (A/B/C), and leaves Fedora as non-blocking. The WebKit audio step fixes the AudioContext suspend hang.

What stays open: none of these steps addresses the performance and robustness defects verified here. After the round:
- an exception in tick/render still kills the loop for good;
- device loss is reported but not recovered;
- Modes A/B still have no tick flow control and no worker restart or degradation;
- startup failures can still leave a blank canvas or a hang;
- the 60 Hz fixed step still has no interpolation;
- every frame still copies and allocates about 128 B per entity;
- the ring buffer is still a fixed 64 KB;
- the texture model is unchanged: 255 square ≤512 px images per tier, no atlas or UV sub-rectangle, no unload;
- mirrored quads are still culled;
- Mode A still has a fixed camera and no textures.

Measured scale on high-end hardware is good: cull 0.04-0.16 ms and sort < 1 ms at 100k; 100k static sprites at 120 fps in Mode B on an M2 Pro; WASM core about 70 KB gzip. Verification still covers only an Apple M2 (Chrome and Safari) and an AMD iGPU on Linux. Net: fine for prototypes and vertical slices on desktop Chrome or Safari with Mode B pinned, not yet for shipping a complete, complex game on web and desktop.

surveys.summary:
On the hardware it was measured on, Hyperion's GPU core scales well. The compute cull takes 42-47 µs at 100k entities on an RTX 4060 laptop and 0.16 ms on an Apple M2 Pro. The GPU transparent sort stays under 1 ms at 100k on both an AMD iGPU and the M2, with a thin margin on the M2. 100k static transparent sprites hold 120 fps in Mode B on the M2 Pro. The 2D lighting costs about 2.3 ms at 1080p on the AMD iGPU and about 4 ms of frame span on the M2, plus about 1.8-2.2 ms for each extra SDF set. The WASM core is about 70 KB gzip, or about 338 KB with physics.

Robustness for shipping a complete game is the weak part, and the open-items round leaves it almost untouched. Step 10 only forwards Mode A device loss and fixes Mode A's empty world. Verified in the code:
- one exception in tick/render permanently stops the loop;
- device loss is not recovered;
- Modes A/B have no tick flow control and no worker restart, so latency can grow without bound;
- startup failures can leave a blank canvas or a hang;
- the 60 Hz fixed step has no interpolation;
- every frame copies and allocates about 128 B per entity in all modes;
- the 64 KB ring buffer ignores its config and makes large spawns flash default quads.

The texture model alone caps a complex game: 255 square images of at most 512 px per tier, no atlas or UV sub-rectangle, no unload. Mirrored (negative-scale) sprites are culled.

Other verified defects: the ECS inspector calls a getter as a method; texture loads are not deduplicated; a full tier reallocates before throwing; KTX2 orientation is never read; ASTC zero blocks; the falloff-0 NaN; light types 5-7; raw ids alias after reuse.

Rust has 347 lib + 100 integration tests (static count). TypeScript has 116 test files (2125 + 7 skipped tests at step 7; the step-8 plan expects 2215). The manual GPU harness has 68 checks. There is no CI. Hardware verification is limited to an Apple M2 (Chrome A/B/C; Safari A/B/C, last run before steps 6-8) and an AMD iGPU on Linux (B/C, up to phase 5b). Windows, mobile, Firefox and desktop shells were never tested.

